 "notice": {
  "head": "Community Radio, by Lantern Light",
  "text": "When an ice storm cut power to Lakeview on a Friday evening, the staff at community station KLVW faced an unusual weekend. Three volunteers had to [[battle against the elements]] just to reach the studio. The station manager, Martin, is [[a man of few words]], but his quiet planning steadied everyone. A retired nurse named Louise [[brought valuable experience to the table]], while a camping stove and two lanterns proved useful [[in a pinch]]. The hardware store and the bakery [[joined forces]] to supply batteries, coffee, and sandwiches. Many listeners had [[grown up on]] KLVW, and they began calling before the first microphone was even switched on.\nWithout computers, the team [[stripped away]] the usual music and polished segments. Martin described the backup transmitter [[in minute detail]], although it would [[stretch the imagination]] to call the setup sophisticated. He joked that he had [[lost a step]] when he fumbled with the old analog mixer, then found the right switch on his second try. The station had [[a devoted following]], but nobody expected the flood of calls. Between updates, Louise usually [[dipped into]] a novel; that night she read every message instead. The volunteers [[stuck with]] a simple schedule: weather, road conditions, then calls.\nAll those emergency drills suddenly [[came into play]]. During a rare quiet moment, one volunteer [[luxuriated in]] a cup of hot coffee. As the wind [[tapered off]], a caller described eating alone in a cold apartment, and her understated account felt [[close to the bone]]. Martin said, “[[Would that it were]] enough to tell everyone to stay positive.” Nobody wanted to [[tap out]], but the night shift finally persuaded him to sleep. By Sunday, the broadcast had become less about surviving without electricity than about who could not safely [[fend for themselves]], and which neighbors were willing to help.",
